From: Eduardo Garcia-Madico Portabella <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 12:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] libcamera: soft: contrast-detection autofocus

The software ISP has no focus algorithm, so a camera whose sensor has a working
focus actuator still takes blurred pictures: applications would have to drive
the lens themselves, and none of them do. Exposing LensPosition is not enough
either, since the controls do not survive the trip through PipeWire.

Add a focus figure of merit to the software ISP statistics - the sum of the
absolute luminance difference between horizontally neighbouring samples, which
defocus blurs away - and an algorithm that drives the lens to maximise it.

Contrast detection can measure how good the current position is but never which
way the focus lies, so the algorithm climbs: it steps, compares against the
previous position, keeps going while the picture improves, and on the first step
that makes it worse turns around and halves the step. That visits far fewer
positions than sweeping the range and ends up finer, since the last steps are
only a few codes wide. It climbs again once the picture has been clearly and
persistently worse than what the last climb achieved, which keeps the lens from
hunting on every wobble.

The figure of merit is normalised by the frame's total luminance, because the
AGC keeps working while the lens moves and an unnormalised sum would rank a
brighter frame above a sharper one.

The lens control travels to the pipeline handler on the sensor's control list,
which the handler peels apart, the same way the IPU3 pipeline handler drives its
lens. The IPA is given the lens' controls alongside the sensor's so it can set
it at all.

